              "text": "Calcium is a vital mineral that your body needs to build and maintain strong bones and teeth, ensure proper muscle function, assist with blood clotting, and regulate nerve signaling. \n\nThe Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) of calcium for most adults is **1,000 mg per day**, which increases to **1,200 mg per day** for women over 50 and men over 70. \n\nCalcium can be found in a wide variety of both dairy and non-dairy foods:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Dairy Products (Highly Absorbable)\nDairy is one of the most common and bioavailable (easily absorbed) sources of calcium.\n*   **Yogurt (Plain, low-fat):** ~415\u2013450 mg per cup (8 oz)\n*   **Yogurt (Greek):** ~200\u2013250 mg per cup (Greek yogurt has less calcium than regular yogurt because of the straining process, but is higher in protein).\n*   **Cow's Milk (Whole, low-fat, or skim):** ~300 mg per cup (8 oz)\n*   **Hard Cheese (such as Parmesan, Cheddar, or Swiss):** ~200\u2013240 mg per ounce (approx. 30g)\n*   **Mozzarella:** ~200 mg per ounce\n*   **Ricotta Cheese:** ~335 mg per half-cup \n\n### 2. Plant-Based & Vegan Sources\nIf you are vegan, lactose intolerant, or prefer to avoid dairy, there are excellent plant-based sources. \n*   **Tofu (Firm, prepared with calcium sulfate):** ~400\u2013800 mg per half-cup *(Always check the packaging to ensure it was processed with calcium sulfate, which drastically boosts the calcium content).*\n*   **Collard Greens (cooked):** ~266 mg per cup\n*   **Spinach (cooked):** ~240 mg per cup *(Note: spinach also contains high levels of oxalates, which bind to calcium and reduce how much your body can actually absorb).*\n*   **Kale (cooked):** ~180 mg per cup\n*   **Bok Choy / Pak Choi (cooked):** ~160 mg per cup\n*   **Chia Seeds:** ~180 mg per ounce (approx. 2 tablespoons)\n*   **Almonds:** ~75\u201380 mg per ounce (about 23 almonds)\n*   **Dried Figs:** ~120 mg per half-cup\n\n### 3. Seafood (Bonus Vitamin D)\nCertain types of fish, especially those eaten with their soft bones, are packed with calcium. These fish also naturally contain **Vitamin D**, which is necessary for your body to absorb calcium.\n*   **Sardines (canned in oil, with bones):** ~325 mg per 3 ounces\n*   **Canned Salmon (with bones):** ~180 mg per 3 ounces\n\n### 4. Fortified Foods & Beverages\nMany foods are synthetically fortified with calcium carbonate or calcium citrate to help people meet their daily goals.\n*   **Fortified Plant Milks (Soy, Almond, Oat):** ~300\u2013450 mg per cup (usually matched to or exceeding cow's milk)\n*   **Fortified Orange Juice:** ~300\u2013350 mg per cup \n*   **Fortified Breakfast Cereals:** ~250\u20131,000 mg per serving (Check the nutrition facts label, as some brands provide up to 100% of your daily value).\n\n---\n\n### Tips for Optimal Calcium Absorption\n1.  **Get enough Vitamin D:** Your body cannot absorb calcium properly without Vitamin D. Make sure you get adequate sun exposure, or eat foods like egg yolks, fatty fish, and fortified milk.\n2.  **Spread it out:** Your body absorbs calcium best in smaller doses (about 500 mg or less at a time). Spreading your calcium intake throughout the day is more effective than consuming it all in one meal.\n3.  **Watch out for \"absorbency blockers\":** Foods high in oxalates (like spinach, rhubarb, and beet greens) and phytates (like raw beans and grains) can lower the rate at which your body absorbs calcium. You can still eat them, just don't rely on them as your *only* calcium sources.",
